From: substrate
Subject: kdrive/evdev: emit one combined motion event per report

EvdevPtrMotion emitted each relative move as TWO events — (dx,0) then
(0,dy) — flushed per EV_REL.  The dix event queue coalesces consecutive
relative motion events, so the earlier X-only event was dropped: under
an X client such as matwm2 (which puts the server under load) the cursor
got stuck moving only on the Y axis, intermittently and at half
resolution.  The kernel input layer was verified correct (mousedbg shows
every report decodes the right dx AND dy), so the loss was purely this
double-emit colliding with motion coalescing.

Accumulate REL_X/REL_Y/REL_WHEEL across a report in EvdevPtrRead and emit
ONE combined (dx,dy) motion event on EV_SYN (plus a flush at end of each
read batch).  A single event per report has nothing to coalesce away.
Also drops the stray ErrorF("abs") debug spew from the absolute path.

--- a/hw/kdrive/linux/evdev.c
+++ b/hw/kdrive/linux/evdev.c
@@ -94,60 +94,49 @@
     }
 }
 
+/* Flush one report's worth of accumulated relative motion as a SINGLE
+ * combined event.  Called on EV_SYN (and at end-of-batch) by
+ * EvdevPtrRead.  `ev` is unused — all state comes from ke->rel[].
+ *
+ * The previous version emitted X and Y as two separate events
+ * (dx,0) then (0,dy) per axis.  The dix event queue coalesces
+ * consecutive relative motion events, so the earlier X-only event was
+ * dropped, leaving the cursor stuck on the Y axis at half resolution
+ * once a client (matwm2) put the server under load.  One combined
+ * (dx,dy) event per report has nothing to coalesce away. */
 static void
 EvdevPtrMotion(KdPointerInfo * pi, struct input_event *ev)
 {
     Kevdev *ke = pi->driverPrivate;
-    int i;
     int flags = KD_MOUSE_DELTA | pi->buttonState;
+    int dx = ke->rel[REL_X];
+    int dy = ke->rel[REL_Y];
+    int wheel = (ke->max_rel >= REL_WHEEL) ? ke->rel[REL_WHEEL] : 0;
+    int i;
+
+    (void) ev;
+
+    if (dx || dy)
+        KdEnqueuePointerEvent(pi, flags, dx, dy, 0);
